 "drivingQuestion": "Whence that the assembly 'there' must all be fit to rule?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Rav Ḥisda's verse (challenged in the next unit)",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "horayot.4b.14",
   "daf": "4b",
   "sentenceId": 13,
   "he": "נֶאֱמַר שָׁם ״עֵדָה״ וְנֶאֱמַר כָּאן ״עֵדָה״, עַד שֶׁיְּהוּ כּוּלָּן רְאוּיִן לְהוֹרָאָה. וְהָתָם מְנָלַן? דְּאָמַר רַב חִסְדָּא אָמַר קְרָא: ״וְהִתְיַצְּבוּ שָׁם עִמָּךְ״ – ״עִמָּךְ״ בְּדוֹמִין לָךְ.",
   "en": "The mishna teaches: «“Assembly” is stated there and “assembly” is stated here…»the court will not be liable «unless all» the judges «will be fit to issue rulings.» The Gemara asks: «And there, from where do we» derive that all the judges must be fit to issue rulings? The Gemara answers that this is «as Rav Ḥisda says» that «the verse states» in connection with the transfer of the Divine Spirit from Moses to the Elders: «“That they may stand there with you”» (Numbers 11:16). The term «“with you”» is explained to mean: «With similarity to you,» in the sense that they must be fit to issue rulings."
  }
 ],
